WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:07.685
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.687 --> 00:00:10.032
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.032 --> 00:00:12.403
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.405 --> 00:00:14.708
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.709 --> 00:00:17.056
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:19.213
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:20.936
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:23.850
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:23.852 --> 00:00:26.756
time for part two of our mini-series on

00:00:26.788 --> 00:00:29.662
three cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.574
In part one, we saw how Edinburgh lost

00:00:32.576 --> 00:00:35.319
its parliament and then reinvented itself

00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:37.771
as the intellectual capital of the world.

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:40.210
In part three, we'll finish in

00:00:40.212 --> 00:00:42.320
Bristol, the city that opened

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.840
England's door to the Atlantic, grew

00:00:44.842 --> 00:00:47.400
rich on the slave trade, and is still

00:00:47.459 --> 00:00:49.444
arguing about that history today.

00:00:49.784 --> 00:00:52.700
But today, we are moving to the city

00:00:52.702 --> 00:00:55.697
where all those clever Scottish ideas

00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:58.925
about markets and machines turned into

00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:01.898
smoke, noise and money: Manchester,

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:05.094
the first industrial city on Earth.

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:07.930
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:07.932 --> 00:01:10.359
minute and get right into it.

00:01:11.881 --> 00:01:14.674
When you first think of Manchester,

00:01:14.676 --> 00:01:17.387
perhaps a few things come to mind.

00:01:18.071 --> 00:01:20.425
If you're a football fan, you might

00:01:20.427 --> 00:01:22.712
think of its two clubs: Manchester

00:01:22.716 --> 00:01:24.814
United and Manchester City.

00:01:25.581 --> 00:01:28.587
If you're a music fan, you probably

00:01:28.589 --> 00:01:31.507
know that bands such as Oasis, The

00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:33.946
Smiths, Joy Division and The Stone

00:01:33.948 --> 00:01:36.098
Roses are all from Manchester.

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:39.521
It's somewhere between the fifth and

00:01:39.523 --> 00:01:41.586
the seventh largest city in the UK,

00:01:41.813 --> 00:01:44.616
depending on how you count it, but

00:01:44.618 --> 00:01:47.419
it packs a serious cultural punch.

00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:51.123
For most of its history, though,

00:01:51.125 --> 00:01:54.178
nobody outside the north of England had

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.449
much reason to think about it at all.

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:59.850
Then, in the space of a single

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:02.363
lifetime, it became the most

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:04.413
talked-about city on the planet.

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:08.992
In the summer of 1835, a young

00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:11.328
French aristocrat stepped

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.363
off a coach in Manchester.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.310
His name was Alexis de Tocqueville.

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:20.632
He was touring Britain to understand

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:22.872
the forces that were changing the

00:02:22.873 --> 00:02:26.751
world, and everyone had told him the

00:02:26.753 --> 00:02:30.630
same thing: you must see Manchester.

00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:34.444
What he found astonished him.

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.654
Huge six-storey factories,

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:40.219
wreathed in black smoke.

00:02:40.705 --> 00:02:42.580
Crowds of pale workers

00:02:42.582 --> 00:02:45.307
streaming through muddy streets.

00:02:45.923 --> 00:02:49.024
Palaces of industry standing next to

00:02:49.026 --> 00:02:51.953
some of the worst slums in Europe.

00:02:53.299 --> 00:02:55.920
He sat down and wrote one of the most

00:02:55.922 --> 00:02:58.612
famous descriptions of any city, ever.

00:02:59.714 --> 00:03:01.988
"From this foul drain," he wrote,

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.607
"the greatest stream of human industry

00:03:04.864 --> 00:03:07.388
flows out to fertilise the whole world.

00:03:07.718 --> 00:03:11.928
From this filthy sewer pure gold flows."

00:03:13.366 --> 00:03:16.594
To understand how Manchester became that

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:19.660
city, we need to go back to the 1770s.

00:03:20.541 --> 00:03:23.018
It was then a market town in the

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.341
north-west of England, home to

00:03:25.342 --> 00:03:27.598
around twenty-five thousand people.

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:31.246
It had no city walls, no cathedral

00:03:31.248 --> 00:03:33.735
status, no member of parliament,

00:03:34.211 --> 00:03:36.508
and no particular claim to fame

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.657
beyond a busy trade in cloth.

00:03:39.642 --> 00:03:41.914
Then it exploded.

00:03:43.112 --> 00:03:46.459
By 1802 the population had reached

00:03:46.461 --> 00:03:49.216
around ninety-five thousand.

00:03:49.798 --> 00:03:52.507
By the census of 1851, it was

00:03:52.509 --> 00:03:55.123
over three hundred thousand.

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:57.865
Nothing this fast had ever

00:03:57.867 --> 00:03:59.728
happened to a city before.

00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:03.203
There were no plans, no real building

00:04:03.205 --> 00:04:05.809
regulations, and no map that stayed

00:04:05.825 --> 00:04:07.911
accurate for more than a few years.

00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:12.990
What drove it all was cotton.

00:04:14.424 --> 00:04:17.682
Manchester, it turned out, had everything

00:04:17.684 --> 00:04:20.145
the new cotton industry needed.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.770
A damp climate that stopped the

00:04:22.772 --> 00:04:24.731
delicate threads from snapping.

00:04:25.378 --> 00:04:28.400
Coalfields nearby to power the mills.

00:04:28.904 --> 00:04:31.135
A port, Liverpool, just thirty

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.142
miles away, bringing in raw

00:04:33.183 --> 00:04:35.206
cotton from across the Atlantic.

00:04:36.136 --> 00:04:37.634
And it had canals for

00:04:37.636 --> 00:04:39.561
easy, cheap transportation.

00:04:40.688 --> 00:04:43.421
In 1761, the Duke of Bridgewater

00:04:43.423 --> 00:04:46.580
opened a canal to bring coal from his

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.237
mines into the town, and the price

00:04:49.239 --> 00:04:52.129
of coal in Manchester roughly halved.

00:04:52.821 --> 00:04:55.863
Cheap coal meant cheap steam power.

00:04:57.177 --> 00:04:59.149
Then came the machines.

00:04:59.641 --> 00:05:01.501
Spinning machines that could do

00:05:01.503 --> 00:05:03.541
the work of a hundred human hands,

00:05:03.912 --> 00:05:06.830
powered first by water, then by steam.

00:05:08.384 --> 00:05:10.879
Manchester's genius was to put

00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:13.540
all of this together, on a scale

00:05:13.785 --> 00:05:15.878
nobody had attempted before.

00:05:16.776 --> 00:05:18.866
It became the first city in the

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:21.225
world whose whole economy was built

00:05:21.263 --> 00:05:23.284
around steam-powered factories.

00:05:24.302 --> 00:05:27.434
In one district, Ancoats, mills of

00:05:27.436 --> 00:05:30.473
six, seven and eight storeys rose

00:05:30.482 --> 00:05:32.815
side by side along the canals.

00:05:33.929 --> 00:05:36.266
Ancoats is often called the

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:39.210
world's first industrial suburb: a

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:41.680
neighbourhood built for factories, not

00:05:41.682 --> 00:05:44.002
for people, though tens of thousands

00:05:44.002 --> 00:05:45.775
of people lived there all the same.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.866
By the second half of the century,

00:05:49.868 --> 00:05:52.657
Manchester and the mill towns around

00:05:52.658 --> 00:05:55.324
it were processing roughly a third

00:05:55.326 --> 00:05:57.912
of all the cotton grown on Earth.

00:05:58.872 --> 00:06:00.921
They called it Cottonopolis:

00:06:00.923 --> 00:06:02.312
the city of cotton.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.468
Cloth spun and woven here dressed people

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.742
in Europe, Africa, India and the Americas.

00:06:10.247 --> 00:06:12.833
If you wore clothes made of cotton, there

00:06:12.835 --> 00:06:15.167
was a good chance they had been made,

00:06:15.279 --> 00:06:18.599
in part, in this great industrial city.

00:06:19.781 --> 00:06:21.675
Now, to give you a sense of what was

00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:23.832
actually happening in these cotton mills,

00:06:23.964 --> 00:06:27.300
if you were to step inside one, the first

00:06:27.302 --> 00:06:30.473
thing that would hit you was the noise.

00:06:31.256 --> 00:06:34.373
Hundreds of machines hammering away

00:06:34.375 --> 00:06:37.401
in one vast room, hour after hour.

00:06:38.322 --> 00:06:41.099
Many workers went partly deaf, and

00:06:41.101 --> 00:06:44.040
mill workers developed their own way

00:06:44.041 --> 00:06:46.800
of communicating: an exaggerated,

00:06:46.802 --> 00:06:50.061
silent mouthing of words, so they could

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.915
lip-read one another over the roar.

00:06:54.183 --> 00:06:57.237
The air was kept hot and damp,

00:06:57.239 --> 00:06:59.985
because warm, moist air was

00:06:59.992 --> 00:07:01.662
better for the cotton thread.

00:07:02.632 --> 00:07:04.342
Temperatures inside could

00:07:04.344 --> 00:07:05.779
reach thirty degrees.

00:07:06.433 --> 00:07:09.615
The air was also thick with cotton

00:07:09.617 --> 00:07:12.703
dust, and these fine white fibres

00:07:13.044 --> 00:07:15.610
would be breathed in by workers,

00:07:15.612 --> 00:07:18.096
settle in their lungs and, over

00:07:18.096 --> 00:07:20.012
the years, slowly destroy them.

00:07:21.295 --> 00:07:23.249
The working day was twelve

00:07:23.251 --> 00:07:25.578
hours or more, six days a week.

00:07:26.877 --> 00:07:30.017
And the machines did not forgive mistakes.

00:07:30.573 --> 00:07:32.154
Fingers and hands would

00:07:32.156 --> 00:07:33.804
be lost in the machines.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.683
And if you were really unlucky,

00:07:36.685 --> 00:07:38.492
incidents could be fatal.

00:07:39.974 --> 00:07:42.226
And everywhere, there were children.

00:07:43.004 --> 00:07:44.946
Small children were prized in the

00:07:44.948 --> 00:07:46.712
mills because they were small.

00:07:47.595 --> 00:07:50.202
They worked as scavengers, crawling

00:07:50.204 --> 00:07:52.734
underneath the moving machinery to

00:07:52.734 --> 00:07:55.061
sweep up the loose cotton, while the

00:07:55.063 --> 00:07:57.647
machines thundered on above their heads.

00:07:58.589 --> 00:08:01.385
Some started at seven or eight years old,

00:08:01.387 --> 00:08:03.976
and of course they were just as likely

00:08:03.976 --> 00:08:06.662
to lose a finger, or even be killed if

00:08:06.664 --> 00:08:09.349
their clothes got caught in the works.

00:08:11.216 --> 00:08:13.077
Outside the factory gates,

00:08:13.079 --> 00:08:15.009
things weren't much better.

00:08:15.591 --> 00:08:18.110
Housing was thrown up as fast and

00:08:18.112 --> 00:08:21.087
cheaply as possible, and whole families

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:23.973
lived in single damp cellar rooms.

00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:28.203
In the worst districts, one toilet

00:08:28.205 --> 00:08:30.532
might serve dozens of families,

00:08:30.769 --> 00:08:33.895
and drinking water came from rivers

00:08:33.897 --> 00:08:36.932
that ran black with dye and waste.

00:08:38.857 --> 00:08:41.574
In 1842, a government report put

00:08:41.576 --> 00:08:44.545
numbers on what this did to people.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.831
Among Manchester's labourers

00:08:47.833 --> 00:08:50.113
and their families, the average

00:08:50.149 --> 00:08:53.664
age at death was just seventeen.

00:08:54.859 --> 00:08:56.899
Now, this is dragged down by

00:08:56.901 --> 00:08:58.867
deaths in childhood, but it

00:08:58.867 --> 00:09:00.294
still gives you some idea.

00:09:01.182 --> 00:09:03.790
In the rural county of Rutland, the

00:09:03.792 --> 00:09:06.398
equivalent figure was thirty-eight,

00:09:06.651 --> 00:09:08.123
more than twice as high.

00:09:09.595 --> 00:09:11.967
Conditions were awful, and they

00:09:11.969 --> 00:09:14.185
were plain for anyone to see.

00:09:15.781 --> 00:09:19.244
In December 1842, a twenty-two-year-old

00:09:19.246 --> 00:09:21.642
German arrived in the city.

00:09:22.305 --> 00:09:24.756
His father was a wealthy cotton

00:09:24.758 --> 00:09:27.682
manufacturer and had sent him to work

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.246
in the office of the family firm,

00:09:30.248 --> 00:09:33.178
partly to knock some radical political

00:09:33.178 --> 00:09:35.322
ideas out of the young man's head.

00:09:36.802 --> 00:09:38.959
His time in Manchester had

00:09:38.961 --> 00:09:41.447
precisely the opposite effect.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:45.085
His name was Friedrich Engels.

00:09:46.584 --> 00:09:49.220
By day, he worked as a respectable

00:09:49.222 --> 00:09:52.089
clerk in the family business, shaking

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.379
hands with mill owners and merchants.

00:09:55.519 --> 00:09:58.457
In his free time, he walked the slums,

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:01.086
taking notes on everything he saw.

00:10:01.849 --> 00:10:05.257
The result was a book which he published

00:10:05.259 --> 00:10:08.325
in 1845 with the title The Condition

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:09.972
of the Working Class in England.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.145
It was a furious, very detailed

00:10:13.147 --> 00:10:15.535
catalogue of how Manchester's

00:10:15.538 --> 00:10:17.793
workers lived and died.

00:10:19.077 --> 00:10:21.261
Engels sent his findings to a

00:10:21.263 --> 00:10:23.444
friend of his, another German

00:10:23.445 --> 00:10:25.809
thinker, called Karl Marx.

00:10:27.142 --> 00:10:29.889
And as you may know, three years

00:10:29.891 --> 00:10:32.206
later the two men would put

00:10:32.235 --> 00:10:34.539
their thoughts together, with the

00:10:34.541 --> 00:10:37.262
publication of The Communist Manifesto.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.836
Modern communism, in other words,

00:10:40.838 --> 00:10:43.431
has Manchester deep in its roots.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.042
Long before any revolution in

00:10:46.044 --> 00:10:48.710
Russia, its raw material was gathered

00:10:48.734 --> 00:10:50.926
in the slums beside the mills.

00:10:51.886 --> 00:10:54.106
Now, the people of Manchester did

00:10:54.108 --> 00:10:56.259
not need a German philosopher to

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:57.499
tell them something was wrong.

00:10:57.892 --> 00:11:00.038
It was clear to see, and they had

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:02.510
been saying it themselves for decades.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:04.716
And they had been trying

00:11:04.718 --> 00:11:06.076
to do something about it.

00:11:07.086 --> 00:11:10.574
See, for most of this period of explosive

00:11:10.576 --> 00:11:13.382
growth, this world-famous city of

00:11:13.383 --> 00:11:16.100
hundreds of thousands of people had not

00:11:16.102 --> 00:11:18.957
a single member of parliament of its own.

00:11:19.878 --> 00:11:21.209
Ordinary working people

00:11:21.211 --> 00:11:22.483
could not vote at all.

00:11:23.295 --> 00:11:26.369
So when bad harvests and high bread

00:11:26.371 --> 00:11:29.443
prices meant they went hungry, they

00:11:29.444 --> 00:11:32.528
had no legal way to change anything.

00:11:33.517 --> 00:11:35.770
So they organised.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:40.252
And on the 16th of August 1819, on an

00:11:40.254 --> 00:11:44.069
open space in the centre of town called

00:11:44.142 --> 00:11:47.204
St Peter's Field, they held the largest

00:11:47.206 --> 00:11:50.345
political meeting Britain had ever seen.

00:11:51.361 --> 00:11:53.987
Tens of thousands of men, women

00:11:53.989 --> 00:11:56.614
and children walked in from the

00:11:56.621 --> 00:11:58.493
mill towns, dressed in their

00:11:58.495 --> 00:12:00.632
Sunday best, their best clothes.

00:12:01.481 --> 00:12:03.967
They carried banners demanding the

00:12:03.969 --> 00:12:06.234
vote, and poles topped with red

00:12:06.273 --> 00:12:09.014
caps, the old symbol of liberty.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:11.870
The mood, witnesses said, was

00:12:11.872 --> 00:12:14.451
closer to a festival than a protest.

00:12:15.894 --> 00:12:18.521
They had come to listen to Henry

00:12:18.523 --> 00:12:21.558
Hunt, the most famous radical speaker

00:12:21.567 --> 00:12:24.128
in the country, a man known for his

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.762
white top hat and his booming voice.

00:12:27.971 --> 00:12:30.091
The local magistrates were watching

00:12:30.093 --> 00:12:32.393
from a house at the edge of the field,

00:12:32.992 --> 00:12:35.702
and they saw it all very differently.

00:12:36.814 --> 00:12:38.890
To them, this looked like the

00:12:38.892 --> 00:12:40.751
beginning of a revolution.

00:12:41.436 --> 00:12:44.065
They ordered Hunt's arrest, and they

00:12:44.067 --> 00:12:46.401
sent in cavalry to carry it out.

00:12:47.764 --> 00:12:49.810
Some of the horsemen were local

00:12:49.812 --> 00:12:51.923
businessmen in uniform, and some

00:12:51.924 --> 00:12:53.705
of them had been drinking heavily.

00:12:54.462 --> 00:12:56.947
They drew their sabres, their

00:12:56.949 --> 00:12:59.604
curved cavalry swords, and rode

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:02.143
into the densely packed crowd.

00:13:03.398 --> 00:13:04.760
The first victim never

00:13:04.762 --> 00:13:06.308
even reached the meeting.

00:13:06.846 --> 00:13:08.847
A two-year-old boy was knocked out

00:13:08.849 --> 00:13:10.907
of his mother's arms by a galloping

00:13:10.908 --> 00:13:13.041
horse on a nearby street, minutes

00:13:13.043 --> 00:13:15.239
before the charge began, and died.

00:13:16.549 --> 00:13:18.271
In the field itself,

00:13:18.273 --> 00:13:20.510
people had nowhere to run.

00:13:21.233 --> 00:13:23.956
Around eighteen died, cut down

00:13:23.958 --> 00:13:26.406
by the horsemen's swords or

00:13:26.406 --> 00:13:28.774
trampled under the horses' hooves.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.685
Several hundred more were injured.

00:13:32.841 --> 00:13:35.430
And many of the injured hid their

00:13:35.432 --> 00:13:37.862
wounds afterwards, terrified of

00:13:37.863 --> 00:13:39.907
losing their jobs if their employers

00:13:39.909 --> 00:13:41.950
learnt they had been at the meeting.

00:13:43.597 --> 00:13:45.995
Within days, a radical Manchester

00:13:45.997 --> 00:13:48.102
newspaper had given the event

00:13:48.202 --> 00:13:50.803
the name that stuck: Peterloo.

00:13:51.301 --> 00:13:54.231
St Peter's Field plus Waterloo, which was

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:57.232
a dark joke at the expense of an army that

00:13:57.232 --> 00:13:59.672
had turned its swords on its own people.

00:14:00.968 --> 00:14:03.182
Peterloo shocked the country.

00:14:04.216 --> 00:14:06.494
The government's response was

00:14:06.496 --> 00:14:09.087
not to apologise, but to send the

00:14:09.092 --> 00:14:11.376
magistrates a letter of thanks.

00:14:12.485 --> 00:14:15.777
The event became known as the Peterloo

00:14:15.779 --> 00:14:19.242
Massacre, and its consequences ran deep.

00:14:20.336 --> 00:14:22.473
A young Manchester cotton merchant

00:14:22.475 --> 00:14:24.548
named John Edward Taylor, who had

00:14:24.706 --> 00:14:27.083
witnessed the massacre, decided the

00:14:27.085 --> 00:14:29.257
city needed an honest newspaper.

00:14:30.061 --> 00:14:32.249
In 1821, he founded the

00:14:32.251 --> 00:14:34.151
Manchester Guardian.

00:14:34.795 --> 00:14:37.498
Perhaps you've read it, albeit under

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.276
its modern name, simply The Guardian.

00:14:40.978 --> 00:14:43.347
It's one of the most read newspapers

00:14:43.349 --> 00:14:45.322
in the world, and has a strong

00:14:45.625 --> 00:14:48.368
centre-left, workers-first worldview.

00:14:49.859 --> 00:14:51.830
After the massacre, little

00:14:51.832 --> 00:14:54.028
by little, it seemed like the

00:14:54.028 --> 00:14:55.868
government started to listen.

00:14:57.116 --> 00:15:00.865
In 1832, a great reform of parliament

00:15:00.867 --> 00:15:04.513
finally gave Manchester its own MPs.

00:15:05.203 --> 00:15:07.105
The vote for ordinary working

00:15:07.107 --> 00:15:09.139
people took far longer, another

00:15:09.151 --> 00:15:10.929
century of campaigning, but this

00:15:10.931 --> 00:15:12.485
wasn't unique to Manchester.

00:15:13.634 --> 00:15:17.686
Now, moving forward a few decades, in the

00:15:17.688 --> 00:15:21.837
1860s, the cotton industry faced a crisis.

00:15:22.976 --> 00:15:26.390
In 1861, a civil war broke out in the

00:15:26.392 --> 00:15:29.896
United States, and the North blockaded

00:15:29.925 --> 00:15:32.551
the ports of the slave-owning South.

00:15:33.484 --> 00:15:35.530
After all, all that raw cotton

00:15:35.532 --> 00:15:37.371
had to come from somewhere.

00:15:38.013 --> 00:15:41.040
And that somewhere was the plantations

00:15:41.042 --> 00:15:43.669
of the Americas, picked by slaves

00:15:43.886 --> 00:15:45.974
whose working conditions made those

00:15:45.976 --> 00:15:47.942
of the Manchester factory workers

00:15:48.272 --> 00:15:50.212
look positively Scandinavian.

00:15:51.454 --> 00:15:53.726
But in 1861, the raw

00:15:53.728 --> 00:15:56.225
cotton stopped coming.

00:15:57.464 --> 00:15:59.254
The result in Lancashire was

00:15:59.256 --> 00:16:00.980
known as the Cotton Famine.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.573
Mills closed by the hundred, and mill

00:16:04.575 --> 00:16:07.611
workers and their families went hungry.

00:16:08.858 --> 00:16:11.172
For these workers, a British deal

00:16:11.174 --> 00:16:13.346
with the South would have meant

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:15.750
the cotton flowing again, the mills

00:16:15.752 --> 00:16:18.082
reopening, and bread on the table.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.762
Yet the workers gathered at a great

00:16:21.764 --> 00:16:24.469
public meeting and declared their

00:16:24.476 --> 00:16:27.378
support for the North, for President

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:30.279
Lincoln, and for the end of slavery.

00:16:31.463 --> 00:16:33.931
Lincoln was moved enough to write

00:16:33.933 --> 00:16:36.473
back, addressing his letter not to

00:16:36.473 --> 00:16:39.173
the Queen or to any official, but

00:16:39.175 --> 00:16:41.873
to the working men of Manchester.

00:16:43.206 --> 00:16:45.638
He called their stance an act of

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:48.297
heroism that had not been surpassed

00:16:48.312 --> 00:16:50.705
in any age or in any country.

00:16:51.689 --> 00:16:53.323
Their own children were going

00:16:53.325 --> 00:16:55.069
hungry because of the blockade.

00:16:55.515 --> 00:16:57.495
Yet they looked at the enslaved

00:16:57.497 --> 00:16:59.475
people on the other side of the

00:16:59.476 --> 00:17:02.391
ocean, the people who grew the cotton

00:17:02.393 --> 00:17:04.834
they spun, and took their side.

00:17:06.288 --> 00:17:08.449
There is a statue of Lincoln in

00:17:08.451 --> 00:17:10.820
Manchester to this day, with words

00:17:10.837 --> 00:17:13.124
from that letter carved beneath it.

00:17:14.159 --> 00:17:16.453
Now, moving forward another forty

00:17:16.455 --> 00:17:18.816
years or so, I must tell you about

00:17:18.855 --> 00:17:21.473
another thing Manchester is famous for.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:25.636
In 1903, a Manchester widow called

00:17:25.638 --> 00:17:28.831
Emmeline Pankhurst sat down with her

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.848
daughters and a few friends and founded

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:34.857
the Women's Social and Political Union.

00:17:35.674 --> 00:17:39.113
Their motto was "Deeds, not words".

00:17:40.026 --> 00:17:42.366
The world came to know them as

00:17:42.368 --> 00:17:44.783
the suffragettes, and they took

00:17:44.838 --> 00:17:47.321
the fight for women's votes from

00:17:47.323 --> 00:17:49.804
polite drawing-room petitions to

00:17:49.812 --> 00:17:52.146
hunger strikes and prison cells.

00:17:53.064 --> 00:17:55.243
In fact, one of the first episodes

00:17:55.245 --> 00:17:57.614
we ever made was on the Suffragettes;

00:17:57.735 --> 00:17:59.716
it's episode number 36, if you'd

00:17:59.718 --> 00:18:01.758
like to listen to the full story.

00:18:02.277 --> 00:18:04.648
But in short, the suffragette

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:07.591
movement can trace its roots back to

00:18:07.591 --> 00:18:10.120
Manchester, to Emmeline Pankhurst,

00:18:10.122 --> 00:18:12.500
who was arrested thirteen times.

00:18:13.238 --> 00:18:14.983
There is a statue of her in

00:18:14.985 --> 00:18:17.052
Manchester today, a few minutes'

00:18:17.058 --> 00:18:18.947
walk from St Peter's Field.

00:18:20.217 --> 00:18:23.033
Now, moving to the modern day,

00:18:23.035 --> 00:18:26.131
the factories have fallen silent.

00:18:26.565 --> 00:18:28.958
The last of the great mills closed in

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:31.610
the second half of the twentieth century,

00:18:31.974 --> 00:18:35.265
and for a few decades Manchester looked

00:18:35.267 --> 00:18:38.135
like a city whose time had passed.

00:18:38.811 --> 00:18:41.389
But perhaps its time has come again.

00:18:42.366 --> 00:18:45.588
In July 2026, Andy Burnham, who had

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.363
spent almost a decade as mayor of Greater

00:18:49.364 --> 00:18:51.881
Manchester, became Prime Minister.

00:18:53.263 --> 00:18:55.479
As mayor, he had argued that

00:18:55.481 --> 00:18:57.775
too much power sat in London,

00:18:58.093 --> 00:18:59.684
and not enough locally.

00:19:00.745 --> 00:19:02.841
Since becoming Prime Minister,

00:19:02.843 --> 00:19:04.936
he has opened Number 10 North,

00:19:05.147 --> 00:19:07.564
a Manchester office where he and

00:19:07.566 --> 00:19:09.755
his ministers work regularly.

00:19:10.579 --> 00:19:13.788
So two hundred years after Peterloo, when

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:16.683
this city had no member of parliament

00:19:16.727 --> 00:19:19.270
at all, the Prime Minister of the United

00:19:19.272 --> 00:19:21.558
Kingdom has an office in Manchester.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:25.033
And its permanent home is planned

00:19:25.035 --> 00:19:27.234
for Great Ancoats Street, in

00:19:27.234 --> 00:19:29.003
the old factory district, which

00:19:29.005 --> 00:19:30.715
is where I’ll leave you today.

00:19:31.735 --> 00:19:34.552
Because if you Google "Ancoats"

00:19:34.554 --> 00:19:37.278
now, Google describes it as “a

00:19:37.279 --> 00:19:39.091
happening neighbourhood known for

00:19:39.093 --> 00:19:40.957
its eclectic food and drink scene.

00:19:41.274 --> 00:19:43.432
Scandinavian eateries, Japanese

00:19:43.434 --> 00:19:45.798
teahouses and Mexican restaurants,

00:19:45.895 --> 00:19:48.457
to name a few, sit alongside craft

00:19:48.459 --> 00:19:51.170
breweries and speakeasy-style bars.”

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.534
So, by all means, if you find

00:19:54.536 --> 00:19:56.997
yourself in Manchester, go to

00:19:57.039 --> 00:19:59.798
Ancoats, enjoy the eclectic food and

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:02.403
drink scene, but know that you are

00:20:02.418 --> 00:20:05.211
standing on the foundations of the

00:20:05.213 --> 00:20:08.169
world’s first great industrial city.

00:20:09.861 --> 00:20:11.947
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:20:11.949 --> 00:20:13.968
episode, the second part of our

00:20:13.981 --> 00:20:15.964
three-part mini-series on three

00:20:15.966 --> 00:20:18.139
cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:20:18.271 --> 00:20:20.091
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:20:20.093 --> 00:20:22.015
and that you've learnt something new.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:25.420
Next up, in the third and final part,

00:20:25.422 --> 00:20:28.436
we'll be heading south-west, to Bristol.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.670
It's the story of the little ship

00:20:30.672 --> 00:20:32.392
that found North America, the

00:20:32.392 --> 00:20:34.841
merchants who grew rich on the slave

00:20:34.843 --> 00:20:37.086
trade, the campaigners who fought

00:20:37.116 --> 00:20:39.162
back, and the statue that ended

00:20:39.164 --> 00:20:41.273
up at the bottom of the harbour.

00:20:41.831 --> 00:20:43.992
If you'd like to go deeper into some

00:20:43.994 --> 00:20:46.214
of today's themes, we have an episode

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.678
on the Industrial Revolution, which

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.976
is episode number 150, one on the

00:20:50.981 --> 00:20:53.555
suffragettes, episode 36, and one on

00:20:53.557 --> 00:20:56.273
the divide between the north and south

00:20:56.275 --> 00:21:00.192
of England, which is episode 486.

00:21:01.291 --> 00:21:03.754
You've been listening to English Learning

00:21:03.756 --> 00:21:06.037
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:21:06.313 --> 00:21:09.220
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:21:09.222 --> 00:21:11.897
I'll catch you in the next episode.
